{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the thread-actions design candidate at ad9ca69df7353a88f01127978d2d7c498d102294 (one commit on main c3169d83; only notes/2026-08-22-thread-actions.md, +130). Design-only review against request 15095; nothing implemented or merged.\n\nWhat holds. The four named conditions are met in outcome: direct Done is one report resting on the request with no hidden promise (table row 48 and lines 58-62); Decline is an assertion resting on the request that asks the requester to retire it (row 49); the browser infers neither adoption nor an indirect basis (lines 77-82, and row 55 keeps Propose adoption and Request review both available because the projection does not say whether a decision is adopted); and the browser signs no review verdict (lines 64-69). The note adds no kind, fold rule, authority rule or endpoint.\n\nFour changes before approval.\n\n1. The design's own shape cannot file its ratification rows. Lines 28-32 and 96-99 route every chosen action through the existing composer, which today signs only supersede and state (ui/src/components/Thread.tsx:582-585 at main) and refuses empty text; ratifications are separate one-click ratify acts. Rows 53 (Accept on a report) and 54 (Agree on a proposal) are ratifications, so as written the design either drops them or silently requires a composer change while line 74 says it uses the existing reply shapes. Give the selector a direct-act variant or say the composer changes.\n\n2. Adoption order is inverted. Lines 3-5 say Hugh adopts the reviewed design before a code request is filed, that is, after review. SKILL.md at main (line 349, Notes and decisions) says propose and ratify before requesting review and rest the review request on the ratified proposal as well as the artifact, because adoption filed beside the review connects to nothing. The filing follows the note: request 15095 rests only on request 15088 and artifact 15092, and the projection holds no propose for this note. Line 124 offers the note as a decision, so the rule applies; fix the note's stated order and the filing chain.\n\n3. The reason given for keeping verdicts out of the browser is false for the MCP path. Lines 64-69 say a verdict signs an exact local checkout and the browser has no verified checkout, but the MCP review tool passes NoCheckout true (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1146) and reads the head from the durable artifact row. The real guarantee is admission refusing any verdict-shaped report that lacks the guarded review_path marker (internal/app/admission.go:71-73). Name that guard; a design that names the wrong one invites an implementer to reason around it.\n\n4. Row 53 states no authority condition. Row 54 correctly requires that the proposal names the viewer as a current permitted satisfier; row 53 offers Accept whenever the requester is waiting on an effective report. architecture.md (layer 5 and 7) says the browser offers a ratification only when the projected satisfier on the target statement, checked against the roster, permits it, and names the harm: an ineffective row in an append-only log. Lines 17-18 also omit the per-statement satisfier from the inputs the section may read, though rows 53 and 54 both need it. Add the condition and the input.\n\nRecorded, not blocking: Decline (row 49) is not gated on no live promise while Accept and Done are; the existing proposal-first affordance (Toolbar.tsx:115-133 offers ratify yes or deny on the single proposal a request rests on and suppresses accept) has no row and line 34 removes row shortcuts; Decline, Blocked and Note need an assert composer mode that does not exist today; the citation bound (records.ts CITED_PROPOSAL_LIMIT, required by architecture.md) is dropped from lines 101-104; Decline names only the requester where the author or a ratifier may retire; lines 10-13 misdescribe the current toolbars (they open on focus and touch) and the composer (doAct and Publish also write durable records); lines 84-86 do not say whether the section closes the seven-affordance membership gap recorded in ui/src/lib/authority.ts:34-47; row 55's cell is a sentence fragment; lines 88-90 cite an unmerged presentation a reader cannot find.\n\nArchitecture: layer seven only. The note restates the layer 5 and 7 contract correctly and changes nothing in it; docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update at this head. Where it falls short it understates that contract (row 53, the satisfier input, the citation bound).\n\nSecurity: examined the browser signing boundary (signingRefusal, doAct, the composer send) and Workroom admission. The note signs as no other actor, moves no credential, adds no endpoint, keeps signingRefusal and the publish checks as the final check and forbids a second authority interpreter. Two residuals, both self-inflicted-log risks rather than external exposure: the verdict exclusion is argued from a property the MCP tool lacks while the real admission guard goes unnamed (item 3), and row 53 offers a ratification with no projected-satisfier check (item 4). No unresolved external security defect.\n\nSimplification: drop the outcome-map paragraph (lines 88-90), which the design says it does not depend on; drop or motivate the Note row (line 56), the only row The problem does not ask for; replace the Boundaries bullets that restate architecture.md with a citation, keeping what is specific to this section; shrink table cell 48 to a pointer at lines 58-62.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7621d451619cd5b9b22c5d3d867581cc4acad060","head":"ad9ca69df7353a88f01127978d2d7c498d102294","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c2bf170bf69d46283a4f5fdc5acb7859a65d8a71\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:57cb82d06db48925daeb418df85d97e0b8816638","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}